  "verbalization": "Dextromethorphan, Levallorphan, and Pentazocine are all members of the morphinane alkaloid class, which is associated with the opioid receptor Oprm1. The evidence suggests that these compounds share a common structural foundation, with Dextromethorphan and Levallorphan resembling Pentazocine, and all three being linked to the opioid receptor through binding. The lowest common ancestors of these compounds—such as the Morphinane Alkaloid class and the Agent Affecting Nervous System—highlight a shared mechanism of action involving the opioid system. The binding of Levallorphan and Pentazocine to Oprm1 is supported by multiple paths, indicating a consistent relationship between these compounds and the receptor. The evidence suggests that Dextromethorphan may also bind to Oprm1, potentially through a similar mechanism, reinforcing the role of the opioid receptor in the pharmacological effects of these compounds. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of the opioid receptor in the action of these drugs, with the shared structural features and binding patterns providing a clear, mechanistic link.",
